# Line-ending normalization.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout (or an editor / OneDrive sync that
# rewrites EOLs) can flip the entire working tree to CRLF. `manage.py` and
# the shell entrypoints then fail *inside the Linux container* — the Docker
# build dies at `RUN ./manage.py collectstatic` with
#   /usr/bin/env: 'python\r': No such file or directory
# and `ENTRYPOINT ["/app/start.sh"]` crashes with a bad `#!/bin/sh\r`
# interpreter. core.autocrlf is intentionally not relied upon; this file is
# the source of truth so every clone behaves identically.

# Default: treat everything as text, store as LF in the repo, and check out
# as LF regardless of platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that MUST be LF to execute in the runtime/build container. Called
# out explicitly so the intent survives even if the wildcard above changes.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
manage.py       text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.template      text eol=lf
*.env.example   text eol=lf
.env.*.example  text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf

# Windows-only helper scripts stay CRLF.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Lockfiles: LF, and collapse their diffs (huge, machine-generated).
package-lock.json text eol=lf -diff
uv.lock           text eol=lf -diff

# Binary assets — never apply EOL conversion.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.eot    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.whl    binary
